WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.210
MCP audit trails should make tool-mediated activity understandable.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:08.980
Useful logs include tool calls, selected server, calling user, application identity,

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.970
authorization decision, approval record, resource access event, policy denial,

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.000
server connection event, error, timing, and outcome.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.220
For high-impact actions,

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.000
logs should preserve enough context to reconstruct who requested the action,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.250
what was approved, what arguments were used, and what downstream system responded.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:28.000
Monitoring should look for unusual tool usage, suspicious server behavior,

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.130
data access anomalies, repeated denials, unexpected local file access, unusual API calls,

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:38.000
elevated error rates, approval bypass attempts, or activity outside normal patterns.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.995
The goal is not to drown teams in model transcripts.

00:00:39.995 --> 00:00:44.590
The goal is to expose the security-relevant events that show whether tools and resources

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.000
are being used according to policy.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Logs also create privacy and security risk if they store too much.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
They should avoid unnecessary secrets, tokens, credentials, regulated data,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
personal information, and sensitive user content.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Correlation IDs, resource identifiers, tool names, policy outcomes,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.500
and carefully scoped summaries can often support investigation without storing full

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.995
prompts or full resource contents.

00:01:05.995 --> 00:01:07.635
Logging design should be intentional,

00:01:07.635 --> 00:01:11.066
because incident response is harder when logs are either empty or dangerously

00:01:11.066 --> 00:01:12.311
over-collected.

00:01:12.311 --> 00:01:16.730
Incident response plans should cover unauthorized actions, data leakage, tool poisoning,

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.975
malicious server activity, policy bypass, approval confusion,

00:01:19.975 --> 00:01:21.165
and suspicious resource access.

00:01:21.165 --> 00:01:24.830
Responders need to know how to disable a tool, revoke a token, block a server,

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:28.975
preserve evidence, identify affected users or tenants, review downstream actions,

00:01:28.975 --> 00:01:30.995
and communicate impact.

00:01:30.995 --> 00:01:34.925
MCP security is strongest when monitoring and response are designed before the first

00:01:34.925 --> 00:01:35.930
serious incident.
